+🇮🇱 [עברית](Diversion-he)
+
+# Divert
+
+One button for deciding where to go **now**. **↱ Divert** sits in the footer beside **Comm
+failure** and **Location** — all three are in-flight decisions. On a phone it is the outlined
+**↱** button in the top [strip](Phone-Layout#the-strip), beside the red Comm fail; in flight (Location
+on) it takes the language switch's place there, so the speed, altitude and heading stay whole —
+the language stays in Settings and the globe returns when Location is off. Press it and NavAid:
+
+1. **Finds where you are.** If Location, a recording or the simulator is already giving a position,
+   that is used. Otherwise the button switches Location on and waits for the first fix; while it
+   waits a card offers **Use map centre** or **Cancel**. With no GPS to wait for, the map centre is
+   used and the card says so.
+2. **Lists the nearest airfields** — the closest six by distance (set by
+   `divertListCount`), each with its **magnetic bearing**, **distance**, **time** and **fuel** to
+   reach at the planned cruise (the route's own speed, else the default leg speed; the burn from
+   your [aircraft](Flight-Plan#aircraft-inputs)).
+3. **Re-routes direct** to the one you tap: the route becomes **NOW → field**, a straight
+   track, and the map fits it.
+
+## The card
+
+- While the GPS warms up: **Use map centre** or **Cancel**.
+- The list: each field as **Name (ICAO)** with a line of **bearing · distance · time
+  · fuel**, and its runway length and surface. Nearest first.
+- After you choose: **Diverting to …** with the figures, **Other field** (the list again), and
+  **Back to my route** (restores the plan you had).
+
+## Notes
+
+- The figures are planning estimates at a constant cruise, not a wind-corrected flight plan. For the
+  full nav log, open the [Flight Plan](Flight-Plan).
+- Divert replaces the route, like comm failure; **Back to my route** puts the old one back while the
+  route is still the one Divert drew.
+- Every airfield on the chart is a candidate, civil and military alike — in a real diversion the
+  nearest hard runway may not be a field you would otherwise plan to.
